# Audit - Target B fractional balanced-grain exclusion

**Date:** 2026-09-05  
**Candidate:** `docs/reports/target_b_fractional_balanced_grain_exclusion_2026-09-05.md`  
**Verdict:** PASS under its stated asymptotically coherent balanced-grain hypotheses.  
**Global LEVEL I status:** STILL OPEN.

## 1. Fraction/diameter identity

For a balanced grain with

`E=(2-epsilon)m^2+o(D^2)`

and global retained crossing fraction

`Theta=E/R_*`,

where `R_*/D^2->Delta_2=1/c`, one has

`(2-epsilon)m^2=Theta Delta_2 D^2+o(D^2)`.

Hence

`D^2/m^2=c(2-epsilon)/Theta+o(1)`.

This is an identity, not a dominant-component estimate.

PASS.

## 2. External coarea components do not spoil the balanced grid lower bound

The balanced phase pair lies in one coarea component. Crossings from its trains to trains outside that component belong to the coarea cut. Choosing phase-bin width tending to zero slowly gives phase error `o(1)` while the coarea cut remains `o(D^2)`.

For a fixed positive central trim of a macroscopic balanced grid, TG1 says every genuinely external separator train spends `Theta(m)` cut crossings. Since the total cut has `o(D^2)=o(m^2)` edges, only `o(m)` external separator trains can affect the selected deep diagonal.

Therefore the audited balanced-grid estimate survives:

`D^2/m^2>=3-4sqrt(3)L(epsilon)-o(1)`,

with

`L(epsilon)=6epsilon^(1/4)+4sqrt(epsilon)`.

PASS.

## 3. Fractional grain cap

Combining the exact identity with the geometric lower bound gives

`Theta<=c(2-epsilon)/Q(epsilon)+o(1)`

whenever

`Q(epsilon)=3-4sqrt(3)L(epsilon)>0`.

At `epsilon=0`,

`Theta<=2c/3=0.7351051939...`.

PASS.

## 4. Residual train budget

The balanced grain uses `3m` distinct train vertices. The remaining coarea components have total crossing count

`(1-Theta)Delta_2D^2+o(D^2)`.

Componentwise Mantel gives residual train count at least

`2sqrt((1-Theta)Delta_2)D-o(D)`

because `sum sqrt(e_j)>=sqrt(sum e_j)`.

Also

`m/D=sqrt(Theta Delta_2/(2-epsilon))+o(1)`.

Therefore

`x>=3sqrt(Theta Delta_2/(2-epsilon))+2sqrt((1-Theta)Delta_2)-o(1)`.

PASS.

## 5. Lower-edge calculus

At

`x_0=8/(3sqrt(c))`,

GMC2 gives

`Theta>=0.8142696805...`.

For `epsilon=0`, divide the train-budget right side by `sqrt(Delta_2)` and write

`f(Theta)=3sqrt(Theta/2)+2sqrt(1-Theta)`.

Its derivative is

`f'(Theta)=3/(2sqrt(2Theta))-1/sqrt(1-Theta)`.

The unique critical point solves

`9(1-Theta)=8Theta`,

so

`Theta=9/17=0.5294...`.

Thus `f` is strictly decreasing throughout the relevant interval `Theta>=0.814...`.

Direct substitution gives

`f(8/9)sqrt(Delta_2)=x_0`.

Therefore any `Theta<8/9` in the GMC-forced interval violates the global train budget; a balanced largest grain requires

`Theta>=8/9-o(1)`.

PASS.

## 6. Explicit epsilon threshold

The fractional cap

`U(epsilon)=c(2-epsilon)/Q(epsilon)`

is continuous and strictly increasing for the tiny epsilon range used. Solving

`U(epsilon)=8/9`

numerically gives the first positive root

`epsilon_*=2.3516153...e-8`.

At smaller epsilon, the fractional diameter theorem forces `Theta<8/9`, contradicting the train-budget requirement.

The numerical margin is stable at the displayed precision.

PASS.

## 7. Scope

The theorem applies when the **largest crossing-mass coarea grain is balanced**. It does not yet handle a unique-max or general unbalanced largest grain, so it is not a Level-I closure.

## Verdict

PASS.
